          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is osteoarthritis, and is it the same thing as arthritis?",
          "answer": "The retrieved passages don't give a plain definition of osteoarthritis, so I can't fully answer what it is from them. They do show that it is treated as a distinct kind of joint condition. The ICD-10-CM coding chapter (a US system for classifying diseases, document coding-icd10-chapter-13-musculoskeletal) gives osteoarthritis its own block of codes, M15 to M19. Rheumatoid arthritis and gout are classified separately, under inflammatory conditions at M05 to M14. So osteoarthritis and \"arthritis\" are not used as interchangeable terms in that system.\n\nThe osteoarthritis condition summary (document condition-osteoarthritis) describes how it differs in symptoms. It says prolonged morning stiffness, several small joints affected on both sides of the body, or whole-body symptoms such as fever suggest inflammatory arthritis rather than osteoarthritis. It also says a hot, swollen, very painful joint with fever needs urgent assessment and is not an osteoarthritis flare. Sudden locking or giving way of a joint, or new nerve symptoms along with back or neck pain, also call for prompt assessment. These are the source's examples, not a diagnosis for you.\n\nThe passages do not explain what causes osteoarthritis or how it develops. If you want to know whether a joint problem fits this description, a clinician can assess it in person.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
